meta {
  name: Create textbook
  type: http
  seq: 9
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/library
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  X-School-Id: {{schoolCode}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "kind": "book",
    "title": "NCERT Mathematics, Class 6",
    "description": "The prescribed textbook for the year.",
    "audience": "grade",
    "grade_level_id": "{{gradeLevelId}}",
    "subject_id": "{{subjectId}}",
    "asset_id": "{{assetId}}",
    "language": "en",
    "author": "NCERT",
    "publisher": "NCERT",
    "isbn": "9788174504937"
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 201
  res.body.kind: eq book
  res.body.audience: eq grade
  res.body.status: eq draft
  res.body.owner_person_id: eq {{callerPersonId}}
}

script:post-response {
  bru.setEnvVar("libraryItemId", res.body.id);
}

docs {
  audience is the whole access story for a content item, and it is checked two
  ways. The audience names which anchor must be present -- grade needs
  grade_level_id, section needs section_id, offering needs subject_offering_id,
  owner and school need none -- and sending an anchor the audience does not use
  is a 400, not an ignored field. An item anchored to a section while claiming
  to be school-wide would be readable by everyone and filterable by nobody.

  Then the anchor is checked against what the caller may publish to. A teacher
  can put something in front of the sections and offerings they teach; making
  it visible to a whole grade they do not teach is 403. The collection runs as
  an unrestricted caller, so it never sees that refusal -- it needs a real
  teacher token, the same gap the README describes for row-level narrowing.

  Items are born draft. Nothing reads a draft but its owner, which is what
  makes request 14 a decision rather than a formality.
}
